# SEQUENCE 11 - WHAT HE LEFT

FADE IN:

INT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - MAINTENANCE ROOM - LATER

The two books remain open on the worktable.

Outside the slatted windows --

Children chase each other through the yard.

A woman laughs at something we cannot hear clearly.

A radio changes stations.

Life continuing five feet away.

Malik has made three stacks.

COMPLETED.

REPEATING.

UNREAD.

Ade watches him make a fourth.

ADE
What is that one?

MALIK
Trips.

ADE
Of course it is.

Malik does not look up.

Passports. Receipts. Folded airline itineraries. Nigerian entry stamps photocopied into the backs of ledgers.

He matches one to a maintenance date.

Then another.

Then another.

Kai sits on the floor beneath the window with Solomon's sister's notebooks around him.

Not reading continuously.

Checking beginnings.

End pages.

Inside covers.

Anything addressed to someone.

Nothing.

Malik writes a date on a scrap of paper.

MALIK
He came back six times in four years.

KAI
Seven.

Malik looks over.

Kai holds up an old boarding stub from inside a notebook.

KAI
This one isn't in his ledger.

Malik takes it.

Adds the date.

ADE
This is a deeply unhealthy way to learn your father traveled.

No one takes the joke.

Ade lets it die.

Malik finds a ruled index at the front of another ledger.

BINDING MATERIALS.
BRASS.
WESTERN STONES.
RIVER LEVEL.
INNER FRAME.

He turns pages faster.

MALIK
There.

Kai rises.

A maintenance table.

Intervals measured in weeks and months.

Some crossed out by Solomon.

Some revised.

The final completed entry is dated before Solomon's death.

Below it --

future intervals.

Already ruled.

Already calculated.

Malik traces the next one.

His finger stops.

ADE
What?

MALIK
This should have been done.

KAI
When?

Malik checks the date again.

MALIK
Nine days ago.

Silence.

Kai looks toward the closed inner door.

KAI
Before we got here.

MALIK
After he died.

Kai studies the page.

The next interval beneath it.

Then another.

KAI
He kept writing dates.

MALIK
Yes.

KAI
After he knew he wouldn't be here.

Malik does not answer that.

He turns the page.

Materials.

Quantities.

Storage locations.

A key number.

Everything orderly.

Kai reaches across him.

Turns another page.

Blank.

Another.

Blank.

He takes the ledger.

Checks the back cover.

Nothing.

KAI
Where is the rest?

MALIK
This is the rest.

KAI
No. Who does it?

Malik looks at him.

Kai flips back through the index.

KAI
There's no name.

ADE
Maybe somewhere else.

Kai starts opening drawers.

Not frantic yet.

KAI
He labeled the cloth.

Drawer.

KAI
He labeled the keys.

Cabinet.

KAI
He labeled which fucking stone moved three millimeters.

Malik takes the drawer before Kai can pull it off its runners.

MALIK
Slow down.

KAI
Where is the page that says what happens when he's dead?

MALIK
We haven't read everything.

KAI
He knew he was dying.

MALIK
Kai.

KAI
He knew.

Malik closes the drawer.

MALIK
I heard you.

KAI
Then stop acting like we're missing a folder.

That lands.

Ade looks between them.

Kai puts the ledger down.

KAI
There's nothing for us.

MALIK
There is a room full of records.

KAI
That isn't the same thing.

MALIK
I didn't say it was.

KAI
You were about to.

MALIK
No. I'm saying he left enough to know something was supposed to happen nine days ago.

KAI
And didn't tell anyone who was supposed to do it.

MALIK
Correct.

Kai almost laughs.

There is nothing funny in it.

KAI
That's Dad.

Malik's jaw tightens.

ADE
Don't.

Kai looks at him.

ADE
Don't make him one thing because you're angry.

KAI
I'm not making him anything.

ADE
You are.

Malik returns to the schedule.

ADE
And you're doing the opposite.

Malik looks up.

MALIK
I'm reading a date.

ADE
You're building him a defense out of stationery.

Malik stares at him.

Ade doesn't smile.

ADE
He left all this.

He touches the table.

ADE
And he didn't tell us.

A beat.

ADE
Both happened.

No one improves the sentence.

At the doorway --

Bode.

He has been there long enough to hear the last part.

Behind him, Eno carries a tray with bread, fruit and three glasses of water.

ENO
If dead men could answer questions, funerals would be much cheaper.

She pushes past Bode.

Sets the tray directly on top of Malik's COMPLETED stack.

Malik moves the papers before the condensation reaches them.

Eno notices.

ENO
Good. Your hands still work.

She points at the food.

ENO
Use them.

Kai remains standing.

ENO
You too.

KAI
I'm not hungry.

ENO
That was not the question.

Kai takes a piece of bread.

Bode enters.

Malik shows him the schedule.

MALIK
Did he ever talk about these dates?

Bode reads.

BODE
No.

MALIK
Did he ask you to continue them?

BODE
No.

KAI
Anyone?

Bode thinks.

BODE
Not to me.

Malik points to the missed interval.

MALIK
What happens if this doesn't get done?

BODE
I don't know.

Kai looks at him.

Bode meets it.

BODE
I know that is not the answer you want.

KAI
It's the answer he left us.

Eno puts a glass of water into Kai's hand.

ENO
Your father is not in this room to argue with.

KAI
That's convenient.

ENO
For him, yes.

Ade looks at her.

A tiny exhale that might have become a laugh on another day.

Eno turns to Malik.

ENO
What are you going to do?

Malik looks at the schedule.

Then the inner door.

Then Kai.

MALIK
Nothing yet.

Kai is surprised.

MALIK
We don't touch the shrine. We don't touch that door. We don't move the brass until we know what the missed work actually was.

KAI
And if waiting is the problem?

MALIK
Then guessing is worse.

KAI
You don't know that.

MALIK
Neither do you.

Ade steps between the argument before it becomes the old one.

ADE
Then that's the rule.

Malik looks at him.

ADE
Not yours. Ours.

To Kai.

ADE
Nobody goes back alone.

To Malik.

ADE
Nobody decides alone.

Malik takes that in.

Kai too.

Bode says nothing.

This is not his decision.

Kai looks at the missed date again.

KAI
Nine days.

Malik folds the scrap with the date on it.

Puts it in his pocket.

MALIK
We start there.

Eno picks up the untouched second glass.

ENO
You start with food.

She exits.

Ade takes a piece of fruit.

ADE
I support the new administration.

A small release.

Even Malik almost gives him something.

Then Kai notices --

The unfinished brass fastener on the inner-door sill.

Exactly where it was.

He does not touch it.

CUT TO:

# SEQUENCE 12 - WHEN IT STAYS

EXT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - COURTYARD - NIGHT

The house is full again.

Dinner aftermath.

Plastic chairs.

Children underfoot.

A football match on television inside draws periodic SHOUTS from the men watching.

Generator vibration beneath everything.

Mosquitoes worry bare ankles.

Eno directs plates from one hand to another without appearing to move.

Ade has been recruited into drying dishes.

ADE
I crossed an ocean for this promotion.

ENO
You were unemployed in my kitchen. I gave you purpose.

ADE
Finally. Succession.

Malik looks at him from the table.

Not amused by that word yet.

Ade catches it.

ADE
Bad one.

He goes back to the dishes.

Kai sits near the open courtyard door with his camera in his lap.

He is not looking through it.

For once.

A LITTLE GIRL, six, draws on the back of an old church program beside him.

She shows Kai a house with five windows.

LITTLE GIRL
This one is yours.

KAI
Why is mine small?

LITTLE GIRL
You are far.

Kai smiles.

Then --

A metallic taste floods his mouth.

His smile goes first.

He swallows.

Nothing clears.

His fingers tighten around the camera.

Across the courtyard, Malik sees the change.

MALIK
Kai?

Kai looks at him.

KAI
Don't --

His ears PRESSURIZE.

The football crowd on television stretches into a thin, impossible whine.

Then cuts.

The television is still playing.

Men inside are still reacting.

We cannot hear them.

Kai can.

But late.

A cheer arrives after the players have already reset.

He stands too quickly.

The courtyard tilts.

Not visually.

His body believes it has.

He catches the chair.

Malik is already moving.

MALIK
Look at me.

Kai tries.

His eyes will not converge on Malik's face.

Two Maliks overlap by half an inch.

Then one.

Then two.

KAI
It's here.

Ade hears that.

The joke leaves him completely.

He sets the plate down.

The LITTLE GIRL looks up.

LITTLE GIRL
What is?

Ade crouches immediately.

ADE
Your uncle's dramatic blood sugar.

He offers her the dish towel.

ADE
Take this to Auntie Eno. Tell her I said emergency biscuits.

LITTLE GIRL
She will say no.

ADE
That's why you're negotiating.

The girl runs toward the kitchen.

Ade rises.

His balance shifts under him.

He grips the table.

His hand lands six inches before the edge he sees.

He freezes.

Looks at his hand.

Does not look toward Kai.

ADE
Malik.

Barely a word.

The GENERATOR HUM disappears.

Not fades.

Gone.

Yet the bulb over the courtyard burns steadily.

Mosquitoes vanish from the light.

Kai's breathing shortens.

He looks toward the passage inside the house.

MALIK
No.

Kai doesn't turn.

MALIK
Kai. Me.

Kai tries to obey.

The passage behind Malik is empty.

Then --

A PERCEPTUAL STUTTER.

Not a flicker.

Not darkness.

For an instant, the room presents two incompatible answers.

Empty passage.

And --

SOMETHING impossibly tall occupying it.

Both feel true.

Kai's knees HIT the floor.

He did not experience falling.

His camera cracks against the tile.

MALIK
Kai!

Malik lunges.

STUTTER.

Malik is beside him.

His own body catches up a beat later.

He nearly falls from momentum he does not remember creating.

His breath locks.

He tries to inhale.

Nothing.

His chest will not begin the action.

Malik's eyes widen.

He forces air out first.

Then drags a breath in.

Again.

Manual.

Ade takes one step toward them.

The floor pitches ninety degrees inside his inner ear.

He drops to one hand.

The courtyard remains level.

A woman carrying plates walks past him normally.

WOMAN
Ade?

Ade gets upright before she can follow his eyes.

ADE
Water. Slipped.

He takes the plates from her.

ADE
Kitchen.

WOMAN
You are sweating.

ADE
Calabar has discovered weather.

Weak joke.

He hates that it came out.

She keeps moving.

Kai is staring past Malik now.

MALIK
Don't.

Kai's eyes water violently.

A capillary bursts red across the white of one eye.

STUTTER.

The Entity is there.

Not arriving.

Being FORCED into a shape the passage can hold.

Its height has nowhere to go.

One articulation folds beneath the ceiling.

Another seems to begin behind the wall and finish in front of it.

Its proportions almost resolve --

STUTTER.

Wrong again.

The head occupies a position the neck does not support.

STUTTER.

Closer.

No step.

The wooden passage frame CREAKS.

A physical load settles into the house.

Dust falls from the lintel.

Upward.

For half a second.

Then down.

Kai's nose starts bleeding.

MALIK
Kai. Stop looking.

KAI
I can't --

His tongue has gone numb.

The words distort in his mouth.

KAI
I ca --

He gags.

Saliva floods his mouth.

He turns and vomits onto the tile.

The Entity becomes clearer.

Not more complete.

More *insistent*.

A surface where a face might be attempts depth.

STUTTER.

A human arrangement almost exists.

STUTTER.

It doesn't.

STUTTER.

For one impossible instant, it is the most spatially coherent thing in the passage.

Everything around it doubles.

Doorframe.

Wall.

Malik.

Kai.

The Entity alone holds one position.

Ade sees enough.

His pupils lose focus.

A high tone tears through his ears.

Then --

LITTLE GIRL (O.S.)
Uncle Kai?

Ade turns.

The little girl stands at the kitchen threshold with two biscuits.

LITTLE GIRL
You called me.

Kai is on his knees vomiting.

He did not call anyone.

From inside the passage --

KAI (O.S.)
Come here.

Perfect.

Same breath.

Same tired Vancouver flattening of the vowel.

The girl turns toward it.

Ade moves.

ADE
Hey.

She looks back at him.

ADE
Those are mine.

LITTLE GIRL
Auntie said one is for him.

ADE
Auntie is testing you.

He reaches her.

Keeps his body between her and the passage.

Behind him --

STUTTER.

The little girl's eyes try to move around Ade.

Ade lifts her into his arms.

ADE
Nope. Tax.

He takes one biscuit with his teeth.

She laughs despite herself.

The laugh is tiny.

Human.

The Entity's coherence falters.

Not because laughter hurts it.

Because one observer has been denied.

Ade carries her toward the courtyard.

He does not look back.

His nose begins to bleed onto her shoulder.

She touches it.

LITTLE GIRL
You're bleeding.

ADE
I know.

No joke now.

At the passage --

Malik grabs Kai's face with both hands.

MALIK
Me.

Kai's eyes keep pulling sideways.

MALIK
Kai.

STUTTER.

For an instant Malik's hands are on Kai --

and Kai is six feet away beneath the passage arch.

Both positions exist.

Then --

Kai is back in Malik's hands.

Kai SCREAMS.

His right arm drops dead at his side.

MALIK
What happened?

KAI
My arm --

MALIK
Can you move it?

Kai tries.

Nothing.

Behind Malik, the Entity resolves one long articulation through the exact space Kai's shoulder occupies.

No contact.

No impact.

Just incompatible geometry.

Kai's body convulses.

Then sensation returns all at once.

He screams harder.

His fingers claw the tile.

Malik turns instinctively toward the passage.

KAI
DON'T.

Malik stops.

That command from Kai saves him from looking.

Inside the house, people are beginning to notice.

A chair scrapes.

ENO (O.S.)
What happened?

Another voice.

BODE (O.S.)
Malik?

More attention moving toward them.

Kai hears it.

His terror changes.

KAI
Get them out.

Malik understands only the immediate danger.

MALIK
Ade!

Ade already has the girl outside.

He sees Eno and Bode approaching from the kitchen.

He steps into their path.

ENO
Move.

ADE
Gas.

ENO
What?

ADE
I smelled gas. Take everybody outside.

Eno looks past him.

Kai retches again.

Malik on the floor beside him.

ENO
Ade --

ADE
Please.

No joke.

No performance.

Eno sees his bloody nose.

That decides it.

She turns.

ENO
Everybody outside.

Immediate household authority.

People complain.

She does not care.

ENO
Now.

Movement.

Chairs.

Children.

Questions.

Each person who turns away from the passage removes a little pressure from the room.

The Entity does not shrink.

Reality around it becomes less damaged.

Kai's double vision begins to settle.

Malik can breathe without commanding each breath.

The passage frame stops creaking.

STUTTER.

Entity.

STUTTER.

Empty passage.

STUTTER.

Something farther back than the house contains.

Then --

Nothing.

Sound SLAMS back.

Generator.

Television crowd.

A baby crying.

Plates clattering outside.

Insects.

Too much at once.

Malik flinches from ordinary sound.

Kai curls around his arm.

Ade stands in the courtyard holding the little girl while blood runs over his lip.

The girl stares at him.

LITTLE GIRL
Was it gas?

Ade looks toward his brothers.

ADE
Yeah.

The lie costs him.

LITTLE GIRL
I didn't smell it.

ADE
Good.

He sets her down beside Eno.

Eno takes one look at all three brothers.

Malik manually flexing his hands.

Kai's bloodshot eye.

Ade's nose.

ENO
Inside the car.

MALIK
No hospital.

ENO
I did not ask you.

MALIK
We can't bring people --

He stops himself.

Too much.

Eno hears the unfinished sentence.

ENO
Bring people where?

No one answers.

Bode stands behind her.

He sees the passage.

Does not look into it.

That choice is not lost on Kai.

Kai tries to stand.

His right arm trembles violently.

Ade reaches him first.

Kai lets him.

Malik gets under the other side.

For once, the three move as one without discussing who carries what.

They reach the passage entrance.

Kai stops.

MALIK
What?

Kai looks toward the maintenance room.

Not at the darkness.

At the RED-CORDED OUTER DOOR.

Still open from the day.

Beyond it --

The INNER DOOR.

The unfinished brass point near the lower hinge --

is no longer empty.

The loose brass fastener that sat on the sill is now seated in the frame.

Not fully tightened.

One turn short.

No one has touched it.

Kai's face drains.

MALIK
What?

Kai cannot answer yet.

Ade follows his line of sight.

Sees it.

His hand tightens around Kai.

The generator hum continues.

Normal.

Normal.

Normal.

CUT TO BLACK.